\description{
Single entry point that turns one propensity score into any combination of
the six tilting-function weights -- inverse probability, SMR, overlap,
matching and entropy weights -- and reports their effective sample size,
weight distribution and covariate balance side by side, so the weighting
scheme can be chosen on evidence rather than habit.
}
\details{
\code{get_PSW()} constructs and diagnoses weights; it does not estimate a
treatment effect. Feed \code{result$data} and the weight column of your choice
to an outcome model, for example
\code{survival::coxph(Surv(t, d) ~ z, data = result$data, weights = w_ato, robust = TRUE)}.
Note that \code{RegR::get_eff_cat()} is \strong{not} a downstream consumer of these
columns: it refits its own propensity model internally, so trimming,
truncation and refitting done here would not carry over to it.
}
\section{Weighting schemes}{

With \eqn{e = P(Z = 1 \mid X)} and tilting function \eqn{h(e)}, the weight
is \eqn{h(e) / P(Z = z \mid X)}: \eqn{h(e)/e} for a treated unit and
\eqn{h(e)/(1-e)} for a control unit (Li, Morgan and Zaslavsky, 2018).

\describe{
\item{\code{"ATE"}}{\eqn{h = 1}; weights \eqn{1/e} and \eqn{1/(1-e)}. Inverse
probability of treatment weighting, IPTW; \code{PSweight}'s \code{"IPW"}.}
\item{\code{"ATT"}}{\eqn{h = e}; weights \eqn{1} and \eqn{e/(1-e)}. SMR
weighting; \code{PSweight}'s \code{"treated"}.}
\item{\code{"ATC"}}{\eqn{h = 1-e}; weights \eqn{(1-e)/e} and \eqn{1}. Also
written ATU.}
\item{\code{"ATO"}}{\eqn{h = e(1-e)}; weights \eqn{1-e} and \eqn{e}. Overlap
weights, OW; \code{PSweight}'s \code{"overlap"}. Balances the covariate means of
the two arms exactly when the score is a logistic fit on those
covariates, and minimises the variance of the weighted contrast.}
\item{\code{"ATM"}}{\eqn{h = \min(e, 1-e)}. Matching weights; \code{PSweight}'s
\code{"matching"}.}
\item{\code{"EW"}}{\eqn{h = -(e \ln e + (1-e) \ln(1-e))}. Entropy weights,
also written ATEN; \code{PSweight}'s \code{"entropy"}.}
}

Only those six names are accepted; the aliases are listed for orientation,
not as arguments.
}

\section{Agreement with WeightIt}{

For the default \code{method = "glm"} the score does not depend on the estimand,
so every weight here is bit-identical to \code{WeightIt::weightit()}'s own: ATE,
ATT, ATC, ATO and ATM all agree to 1.8e-15, in both arms and without any
rescaling. \code{"EW"} has no WeightIt counterpart.

The score-adaptive backends are a different matter. \code{"cbps"} fits the score
so as to balance for a \emph{particular} estimand, and \code{"gbm"} tunes its trees
against an estimand-specific criterion, so their \verb{$ps} changes with
\code{estimand} (measured here: 3.8e-02 for cbps, 4.6e-01 for gbm). \code{get_PSW()}
fits the score once, with \code{estimand = "ATE"}, and shares it across every
tilting function -- weight columns built on six different scores could not
be compared, and \code{"EW"} has no score of its own to fit against. So with
these backends \code{get_PSW(method = "cbps")} is deliberately \emph{not} the same
estimator as \code{WeightIt::weightit(method = "cbps", estimand = "ATO")}.

\code{"gbm"} is also stochastic: two identical calls differ unless you
\code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} first.
}

\section{Order of operations}{

The score is fitted (or taken from \code{ps}), then trimmed, then optionally
refitted on the retained units, then truncated, and only then turned into
weights; stabilisation is applied last. Every estimand shares that one
processed score, because weight columns built on different analysis
populations could not be compared.
}

